# 2. The Architecture

The final architecture looks like this:

```text
                        INTERNET
                            |
                            |
                         GitHub
                            |
                            | Git Checkout
                            ↓
                    ┌─────────────────┐
                    │      VM1        │
                    │ Jenkins Server  │
                    │                 │
                    │ Maven           │
                    │ Docker          │
                    │ Git             │
                    │ SSH Client      │
                    └────────┬────────┘
                             |
                             | Build Application
                             |
                             ↓
                     Docker Image Build
                             |
                             ↓
                       Docker Hub
                    ┌─────────────────┐
                    │   Docker Hub    │
                    │ Container Image  │
                    └────────┬────────┘
                             |
                             | docker pull
                             |
                             ↓
                    ┌─────────────────┐
                    │      VM2        │
                    │ Deployment      │
                    │ Server          │
                    │                 │
                    │ Docker Engine   │
                    │ SSH Server      │
                    └────────┬────────┘
                             |
                             | Port 3080
                             ↓
                         Browser
```

The important concept is that **VM2 represents the role of an EC2 deployment server**.

In a real AWS environment:

```text
Jenkins → EC2
```

In our local environment:

```text
Jenkins VM1 → Deployment VM2
```

The DevOps principles remain essentially the same.

---

# 3. Why Use Two Virtual Machines?

Using two VMs gives us a more realistic DevOps environment than running everything on one machine.

## VM1 — CI/Build Server

VM1 is responsible for:

* Jenkins
* Git
* Maven
* Java
* Docker
* SSH client
* Docker Hub authentication
* Building the application
* Building the Docker image
* Pushing the image to Docker Hub
* Connecting to VM2 through SSH
* Triggering deployment

VM1 represents our:

> Jenkins/CI server

---

## VM2 — Deployment Server

VM2 is responsible for:

* Running Docker
* Receiving deployment commands from VM1
* Pulling Docker images
* Running application containers
* Exposing the application to the network

VM2 represents our:

> AWS EC2 application/deployment server

---

# 4. VMware Network Configuration

Both virtual machines were configured inside VMware Workstation.

The machines were placed on a network that allowed them to communicate.

For example:

```text
VM1
192.168.146.x

        |
        | VMware Network
        |
        ↓

VM2
192.168.146.138
```

We verified the IP address of each VM using:

```bash
hostname -I
```

or:

```bash
ip addr
```

The private IP address of VM2 was particularly important because Jenkins on VM1 needed to connect to VM2.

---

# 5. Testing VM-to-VM Communication

Before configuring Jenkins, we confirmed that VM1 could communicate with VM2.

From VM1:

```bash
ping -c 4 192.168.146.138
```

If the ping succeeds, the machines can communicate at the network level.

We then tested SSH connectivity.

```bash
ssh username@192.168.146.138
```

This is important because the eventual Jenkins deployment process depends on SSH.

---

# 6. Configuring SSH Between VM1 and VM2

The deployment process requires Jenkins on VM1 to communicate with VM2.

The SSH model was:

```text
VM1
Jenkins
  |
  | SSH
  ↓
VM2
Deployment Server
```

An SSH key pair can be generated on VM1:

```bash
ssh-keygen -t ed25519
```

This produces:

```text
~/.ssh/id_ed25519
~/.ssh/id_ed25519.pub
```

The private key remains on VM1.

The public key is placed on VM2 in:

```text
~/.ssh/authorized_keys
```

This allows VM1 to authenticate to VM2 without requiring an interactive password.

The connection can then be tested:

```bash
ssh username@192.168.146.138
```

Once passwordless SSH is working manually, Jenkins can use the same mechanism.

