# 9. Jenkins Pipeline

The Jenkins pipeline becomes the central automation engine.

The high-level pipeline was:

```text
Checkout
   ↓
Build Application
   ↓
Build Docker Image
   ↓
Push Docker Image
   ↓
SSH to VM2
   ↓
Pull Docker Image
   ↓
Stop Previous Container
   ↓
Run New Container
   ↓
Expose Application
```

This is the core CI/CD process.

---

# 10. Stage 1 — Checkout Code

Jenkins first retrieves the source code from Git.

Conceptually:

```text
GitHub
   ↓
Jenkins
   ↓
Workspace
```

The Jenkins workspace contains the application source code.

For a Maven Java application, this might include:

```text
pom.xml
src/
Dockerfile
Jenkinsfile
```

---

# 11. Stage 2 — Build the Application

After checkout, Jenkins builds the application using Maven.

For example:

```bash
mvn clean package
```

Maven performs several activities:

```text
Clean previous build
       ↓
Compile source code
       ↓
Run tests
       ↓
Package application
       ↓
Generate JAR
```

For example, the resulting artifact could be:

```text
target/demo-app-1.1.9.jar
```

This represents the application artifact produced by the CI process.

---

# 12. Stage 3 — Build the Docker Image

The next step is to package the application into a Docker image.

The Dockerfile defines how the application should be packaged.

A simplified example:

```dockerfile
FROM eclipse-temurin:21-jdk

WORKDIR /app

COPY target/demo-app-1.1.9.jar app.jar

EXPOSE 8080

ENTRYPOINT ["java", "-jar", "app.jar"]
```

Jenkins then executes something similar to:

```bash
docker build -t azubuike1/demo-app:1.1.9-6 .
```

Docker reads the Dockerfile and produces an image.

The result is:

```text
azubuike1/demo-app:1.1.9-6
```

---

# 13. Understanding the Docker Image

The Docker image contains everything necessary to run the application:

```text
Docker Image
│
├── Java Runtime
├── Application JAR
├── Application configuration
└── Runtime instructions
```

This gives us an important deployment principle:

> Build once and deploy the same artifact.

Instead of rebuilding the application on VM2, VM2 simply receives the already-built image.

---

# 14. Stage 4 — Push Image to Docker Hub

After successfully building the image, Jenkins authenticates to Docker Hub.

The image is then pushed:

```bash
docker push azubuike1/demo-app:1.1.9-6
```

The architecture becomes:

```text
VM1
Jenkins
   |
   | docker push
   ↓
Docker Hub
   |
   ↓
azubuike1/demo-app:1.1.9-6
```

Docker Hub therefore acts as the **container image registry**.

In a larger production environment, this could instead be:

* Amazon ECR
* GitHub Container Registry
* GitLab Container Registry
* Harbor
* Nexus Repository

---

# 15. Why Docker Hub Is Important

The registry creates separation between:

```text
BUILD
```

and:

```text
DEPLOYMENT
```

VM1 builds the image.

Docker Hub stores the image.

VM2 retrieves the image.

Therefore:

```text
VM1
BUILD
  |
  ↓
Docker Hub
REGISTRY
  |
  ↓
VM2
DEPLOY
```

This is a very important CI/CD architecture.

---

# 16. Stage 5 — Connect to VM2

After the image has been pushed successfully, Jenkins connects to VM2 using SSH.

Conceptually:

```text
Jenkins VM1
     |
     | SSH
     ↓
VM2
```

The Jenkins SSH credentials contain the private key required to authenticate against VM2.

Jenkins can then execute remote commands.

For example:

```bash
ssh user@192.168.146.138
```

---

# 17. Stage 6 — Pull the Docker Image on VM2

Once connected to VM2, Jenkins instructs Docker to pull the image from Docker Hub:

```bash
docker pull azubuike1/demo-app:1.1.9-6
```

The flow is:

```text
Docker Hub
     |
     | docker pull
     ↓
VM2 Docker Engine
```

VM2 does not need to compile the Java application.

It simply retrieves the ready-made image.

This is one of the major advantages of container-based deployment.

---

# 18. Stage 7 — Run the Container

After pulling the image, VM2 starts the container.

For example:

```bash
docker run -d \
  --name demo-app \
  -p 3080:8080 \
  azubuike1/demo-app:1.1.9-6
```

The important part is:

```text
-p 3080:8080
```

This means:

```text
VM2 Port 3080
      ↓
Container Port 8080
```

The application is therefore accessible through VM2's IP address.

---

# 19. Understanding Docker Port Mapping

Suppose VM2 has:

```text
192.168.146.138
```

and the container uses:

```text
8080
```

while Docker publishes:

```text
3080:8080
```

The complete flow is:

```text
Browser
   |
   | http://192.168.146.138:3080
   ↓
VM2
Port 3080
   |
   | Docker NAT/port forwarding
   ↓
Container
Port 8080
   |
   ↓
Java Application
```

This was the final step that allowed the deployed application to be viewed through the browser.

---

# 20. Verifying the Running Container

On VM2:

```bash
docker ps
```

We eventually obtained a running container similar to:

```text
CONTAINER ID   IMAGE                       PORTS
3e070dd9fe49   azubuike1/demo-app:1.1.9-6  0.0.0.0:3080->3080/tcp
```

The important information is:

```text
0.0.0.0:3080->3080/tcp
```

This tells us that Docker published port 3080 on VM2.

If the application itself listens on port 8080, the more appropriate mapping would be:

```text
0.0.0.0:3080->8080/tcp
```

and the container would be started using:

```bash
docker run -d \
  -p 3080:8080 \
  --name demo-app \
  azubuike1/demo-app:1.1.9-6
```

---

# 21. Testing the Application

Before using the browser, we can test from VM2:

```bash
curl http://localhost:3080
```

If the application responds, the container and application are working.

We can then open the application from the host computer:

```text
http://192.168.146.138:3080
```

The browser communicates with VM2, Docker forwards the request to the container, and the Java application responds.

---

# 22. Firewall Configuration

If the application works from VM2 but cannot be accessed from the host computer, the firewall may be blocking port 3080.

Check:

```bash
sudo ufw status
```

If necessary:

```bash
sudo ufw allow 3080/tcp
```

Then verify:

```bash
sudo ufw status
```

The security model is therefore:

```text
Port 22
   ↓
SSH
   ↓
Allow Jenkins/VM1

Port 3080
   ↓
Application
   ↓
Allow required client/network access
```

