# 26. Why This Approach Is Valuable

This laboratory removes one of the biggest barriers to DevOps learning: **cloud infrastructure cost**.

A beginner does not necessarily need an AWS account to understand:

* CI/CD
* Jenkins pipelines
* Git integration
* Maven builds
* Docker image creation
* Container registries
* SSH deployment
* Remote server deployment
* Linux firewall concepts
* Docker networking
* Application exposure

These concepts can first be learned locally.

Once the learner understands the architecture, moving from:

```text
VM2
```

to:

```text
AWS EC2
```

becomes much easier.

The major difference becomes infrastructure configuration rather than completely learning a new deployment process.

---

# 27. What Would Change When Moving to AWS?

Our local deployment:

```text
Jenkins VM1
     |
     | SSH
     ↓
VM2
```

could later become:

```text
Jenkins
     |
     | SSH
     ↓
AWS EC2
```

The Docker deployment commands can remain very similar:

```bash
docker pull IMAGE
docker stop CONTAINER
docker rm CONTAINER
docker run ...
```

What changes are mainly:

* EC2 provisioning
* VPC/subnet
* Security Groups
* Public/private IP addressing
* AWS key pairs
* Internet Gateway/NAT configuration
* IAM permissions
* ECR instead of Docker Hub, if desired
* DNS/domain configuration

Therefore, the local lab provides a strong foundation before moving into AWS.

