# 31. Conclusion

This VMware-based laboratory demonstrates that meaningful DevOps and CI/CD experience does not have to begin with expensive cloud infrastructure.

By using two local virtual machines, we created a realistic separation between:

```text
CI / BUILD
```

and:

```text
CD / DEPLOYMENT
```

VM1 performed the role of the automation/build server, while VM2 performed the role of the application deployment server normally represented by AWS EC2.

The complete pipeline successfully followed the modern deployment pattern:

```text
Git
 ↓
Checkout
 ↓
Maven Build
 ↓
Docker Build
 ↓
Docker Image
 ↓
Docker Hub
 ↓
SSH
 ↓
VM2 / EC2 Simulation
 ↓
Docker Pull
 ↓
Docker Run
 ↓
Port Mapping
 ↓
Browser
```

The key lesson is not simply how to run an application on two VMs.

The real lesson is understanding **how the different DevOps components communicate and how a CI/CD pipeline moves an application from source code to a running production-like environment**.

Once this architecture is understood locally, transitioning to AWS EC2 becomes an infrastructure exercise rather than starting the entire DevOps journey from zero.

> **Build locally. Understand the architecture. Automate the process. Then move to the cloud when the resources are available.**
